<header><p class="st-kicker">crusader state · Anatolia and the Levant</p><h1 class="st-title">Lordship of Beirut</h1><p class="st-meta">1110 CE – 1291 CE · capital Beirut</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Crusader lordship whose Ibelin lords led the barons of Outremer against Emperor Frederick II.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Baldwin I took <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/beirut">Beirut</a> in May 1110 with Genoese and Pisan ships, and the city became a lordship of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-jerusalem">Kingdom of Jerusalem</a>, first held by Fulk of Guines and later by the Brisebarre family.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/beirut">Beirut</a> was lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saladin">Saladin</a> in 1187 and regained by German crusaders in 1197. Its most famous lord, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-ibelin-the-old-lord-of-beirut">John of Ibelin, the Old Lord of Beirut</a>, rebuilt the citadel and a palace whose marble and mosaics impressed visitors, and led the baronial resistance to the imperial officials of Frederick II in the 1230s. The city traded with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/damascus">Damascus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyprus">Cyprus</a>, and its lords held lands on both shores.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>After the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/lordship-of-ibelin">Ibelin</a> line ended, <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/beirut">Beirut</a> passed through heiresses. Following the fall of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acre">Acre</a> in 1291, its garrison surrendered to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluks</a> in July and the city was dismantled.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="crusader state, 1110 CE – 1291 CE"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M26 24 H74 V52 Q74 74 50 84 Q26 74 26 52 Z"/><path d="M50 24 V84 M26 46 H74"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">crusader state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Anatolia and the Levant</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:81.79%;width:3.60%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1110 CE – 1291 CE</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/12th-century">12th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>crusader state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Beirut</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>John of Ibelin, the Old Lord of Beirut, John II of Beirut, Isabella of Beirut</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Latin Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Old French</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/lebanon">Lebanon</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Seigneurie of Beirut</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1200&amp;focus=lordship-of-beirut" aria-label="Lordship of Beirut on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5585 949 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Lordship of Beirut lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5905 1205L5908 1277M5905 1205L5815 1335"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5908 1277h0M5815 1335h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5905 1205h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5905 1205h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Beirut. <a href="/atlas?year=1200&amp;focus=lordship-of-beirut">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

